  ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
    ":db/id": 17592186087543,
    ":question/id": "e429a65a-d65d-5b3f-a054-e3ab8a61caf3",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-5299",
    ":question/problem": "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-x-6}.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x .$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$"
  },
