      ":question/id": "34e39715-21d0-5ed5-8865-d1712b1e9cdb",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-39790",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{2{x}^{2}+x-3}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+1}{(2x+3)(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1.$"
    },
